commentBody:
@Lewis 
WEI WEI impressive... that was good! I smell a Saturday clue in there somewhere.

I was pretty pleased with myself for getting Wei Wei, although with a big asterisk of uncertainty.

@Curtis
Opinions seem split on this puzzle.  I can see the cleverness of the thing.  But I am among those who couldn't complete it alone.  I needed fifteen cheats.  And when the grid was filled, I had a heck of a time finding the error.  I didn't catch that the "performances for one" was plural, and so had "solo".  The crossing result, "Are you on" didn't look wrong to me.    

Enough people, however, are loving the thing.  Tomorrow's another day, with a new (and easy) puzzling awaiting us.

@Barry Ancona

I took the entire comment as an opinion, including the last sentence. There's no problem with that. 

Also,  "alone" could mean by oneself, in other words, with no help by revealing letters, looking in dictionaries and so on.

@Joan
I don't think that opinions are split on this puzzle, or any other puzzle. The majority of commenters here are almost always positive, including today, and those who dislike a particular puzzle are often met with pushback from the "lovers." This may deter those who dislike a puzzle from commenting. I've been a victim of that more than once.

And I'm one who completed it alone, recognizing the creativity and complexity but not really enjoying the fill.

@Times Rita

I didn’t particularly like (or dislike) this puzzle, and I certainly don’t mind if other people have critical things to say about it.

But I have a hard time seeing how this puzzle could be possibly be characterized as exceptionally obscure or complex…

@SP

Oh, I thought it was pluralized with an i.... TEPEi, no?  Har! 😏

I jest, that actually gave me pause for a minute or so, as well.

@Steve L, Cc @SP

I must disagree about the singular. When traveling (or among travel industry people) I often hear/use it like, "How's your accommodation?" or, "Did you arrange your accommodation?"

I guess that's why I wondered about the clue.

@Steve L

Indeed, I am not, though I have been accused of it (and worse).

Grew up on the other side of the Hudson from your current location. But I've lived longer outside the US than in it (including a short stint in London), and over the years have palled around with expats of all stripes.

@SP
spoilers! ;)
I'm working through the archives backwards, and am in May of 2016. I'll jump back to January to do that puzzle before I read any more of your comment.

@Chris

I'd suggest you keep ELAN in your crossword pocket. It shows up clued very similarly a pretty fair amount.

It showed up food as zest a week ago and enthusiasm a week before that. You probably got them on the crosses those other times, but it is a good one to remember.

@john ezra

Agreed! Iactually put in CORIOLIc. Ending in a c made a lot of sense to me and seem to follow language logic, and names can be pretty much anything and I definitely didn't know the chef's name, so why not cAMIN.  🤷‍♀️

When I saw the old horse feathers, I went straight there and tried S. And I LIVEON to tell the tale!

@Chris @Heathie @Steve @John

Re Coriolis Effect:  All you need is Newtonian Mechanics plus a smattering of vector algebra applied to rotating bodies.🚀👩🏼‍🚀

There's a story that the British used Northern Hemisphere parameters in the Falkland Islands during a WWI battle causing naval guns to miss their targets.  It even was quoted in some textbooks for years.  In 2022 a thorough search of primary sources proved it to be a myth.

@Matt

Oh man, I took one look at it on my phone and immediately closed it and went to my computer. My eyes were already strained enough.

Hope you get some good sleep in!!

@Steve L

I do maybe half of the Sunday puzzles on my phone without a problem when I feel like lounging more than I want to be at my desk, and I usually do the same thing as you.  I also then turn off the jump to the next word setting, because when I zoom in like that , it's easier to keep working in the same area. 

I'm at my desk a lot during the week for work, so sometimes it's nice to just lounge the long puzzle, but I have a good desk set up too, so it's all good!

@Gregory Melahn
I realise that this is entirely a matter of preference but what leads you (or anyone here) to prefer dark mode? I've tried it and I just don't see what the advantages are.
Can someone provide a tutorial on Dark Mode?

@Red Carpet You're literally arguing that he can't speak for anybody but himself, when

A. He wasn't doing that anyway.
B. You're the one who's actually doing that.

@HeathieJ
I too am trying to abandon using animal metaphors for disparagement. My current halfway house is this version: "I'd call X a lousy creep but why should I disparage a perfectly ordinary insect?"
And when someone calls their enemy "animals",  I respond by saying: No, they're humans and have moral capacity and freedom of choice. Calling them 'animals' lets them off the hook.

@Alex

Was it a TEEHEEHEE!?  ☺️

I really liked that one too, I kept trying to make some version of stylist fit for a bit.

@HeathieJ
Yep it's got me TEEHEEHEEing like an NYT crossword!
I was first thinking it was something to do with charcoal, like you use to draw shaded greyscale pictures, I needed a lot of crossers to get it.

@Eli Edwards

Actually, I don't see anything unusual about the puzzle other than a couple of colored letter.  

What are you seeing?

@jennie

For it to truly resemble that Indian symbol, which is what it really is, it would need those blocks to be L-shaped, and to be precise, interconnected.

Eli has too much imagination.

@Lpr

It’s clues like this that drive me nuts. A 3-letter word, you have 2 of the letters, should be easy to complete, right? But then you run through the entire alphabet and nothing seems right. Sal soda??  Really? (Eventually, PLUM JOB came to me and I got it from that.)

I’d also like to give a shout out to PLUTOED. Never heard that word before, but I guessed it from the clue and am instantly a fan.

@Carlo 

“Merc” and “merk” have always been the standard shortenings of “mercenary.”

I can find no examples of “murk” fitting the bill.

@Matt
I guess when you turn the abbreviation of a noun into a verb the spelling is anything you’d like it to be.
Carlo likes it dim.

@Juliati

SORTER/PECTIN work the same way.

Some think it's more elegant to have real words in trick formations like these, even when there's no clue for them.

@Max

The answer is CORIOLIS.

While both “ita” and “ito” would have worked for that cross, the correct spelling of CORIOLIS resolves the ambiguity.

@Suzanne

Can't say how they speak where you live.  The only thing I know about Albuquerque is that it's where Bugs Bunny should have turned left.  But the expression "give accommodation to" isn't the same thing as referring to a lodging as your "accommodation."

And even if it is, using the plural form for a singular location is fairly typical in the US, so there's nothing wrong with the clue.

@Suzanne
I'm more familiar with the singular usage when referring to other meanings of the word. 

Out of curiosity, checked M-W. For using "accommodation" to refer to lodging, food, etc., it says, "Usually plural" and both examples are in the plural. So I assume the singular is not incorrect, just not very common in the US when referring to lodgings.

@Charlotte K
The one square that tripped me up and blocked the happy music was the "I"where PILE crosses NILE. I had an "O" there. NoLE of course makes no sense at all as a source of papyrus, and I realized that at the time. But since one can drive a PoLE into a ground, and that was the first thing I thought of, I just blocked and forgot about NoLE being a red flag for what could be wrong at the end. That's the kind of thing that can happen. You're sure something's right but there's a better answer, especially going the other way.

Also since the letter O and the number zero keys are so close to one another in a QWERTY keyboard, remember to check all your O's and make sure one isn't narrower than it should be.

@Bruce
Can't believe I didn't know about the National Lampoon Radio Hour. I would have been at UGA at the time. Do you know what stations it played on?

Looks as though all episodes are on the Interweaves. When all my podcasts are too depressing, I'll turn to these.

@Anonymous

Also, re answers like SADIE Sink.  Remember the answers you didn't get.  SADIE has been clued to Ms. Sink (with that exact same clue) five times since 2022.  At some point, you should know it.

I've never watched "Stranger Things," and don't know what Ms. Sink looks like, but it was a gimme for me.

@Steve L

I’m in the same boat as Barry here.

I’ll probably remember Sadie Sink moving forward…but only because of this exchange. If a trivia gap never causes me any heartburn, I can easily “input” the correct answer any number of times without really seeing it (as has evidently been the case with Sadie Sink).

@lucky13

See also: 

<a href="https://store.nytimes.com/collections/apparel/products/phew-wordle-shirt?variant=42179266314310" target="_blank">https://store.nytimes.com/collections/apparel/products/phew-wordle-shirt?variant=42179266314310</a>

@VN

Or just wrong.  US practice is to use the plural to describe one lodging or many; British practise is to use the singular in both cases.

In other words, in the US, a tepee or hotel room is your accommodations; in the UK, it's your accommodation.

@Justin
Agreed re SOLI, but thus like the third time in a month it's been clued that way.
The sax soli is when we trumpets get a swig of beer.

@Tim M
I was so happy to see Tharp mentioned in the puzzle. She lives and works in NYC but is one of the most influential choreographers world-wide. 

Here is a feature article about her in this newspaper from last year: <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2025/10/13/arts/dance/twyla-tharp-american-ballet-theater.html?unlocked_article_code=1.EVE.RWBc.Qr6yJ4ekNV39&smid=url-share" target="_blank">https://www.nytimes.com/2025/10/13/arts/dance/twyla-tharp-american-ballet-theater.html?unlocked_article_code=1.EVE.RWBc.Qr6yJ4ekNV39&smid=url-share</a>

@Vaer
As a fan of all three, I thank you.

(My first reply seems to have dropped into the aether.)
